      "text": "To find the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle, we can use the Pythagorean theorem. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nIn this problem, we know two sides of the triangle, which are 6 cm and 8 cm, and we assume the right angle is between these two sides. Let's denote the length of the remaining side (the hypotenuse) as \\( c \\).\n\nAccording to the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[ c^2 = 6^2 + 8^2 \\]\n\\[ c^2 = 36 + 64 \\]\n\\[ c^2 = 100 \\]\n\\[ c = \\sqrt{100} \\]\n\\[ c = 10 \\]\n\nTherefore, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is \\( 10 \\) cm. \n\nSo the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10.00}\n\\]",
