      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle, we first need to identify the type of triangle we have. We are given that the triangle has a right angle and two sides of lengths 6 cm and 8 cm. Let's denote the two given sides as \\(a = 6\\) cm and \\(b = 8\\) cm, and the unknown side as \\(c\\). Since one of the angles is a right angle, the triangle is a right triangle, and we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find \\(c\\).\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (\\(c\\)) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides (\\(a\\) and \\(b\\)). Mathematically, this is expressed as:\n\\[\nc^2 = a^2 + b^2\n\\]\nSubstituting the given values for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\nc^2 = 6^2 + 8^2\n\\]\n\\[\nc^2 = 36 + 64\n\\]\n\\[\nc^2 = 100\n\\]\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{100} = 10\n\\]\nTherefore, the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is \\(\\boxed{10.00}\\) cm.",
